    "section": "Common Cold versus Influenza (Flu): Symptoms, Differences, and Care",
    "text": "# Common Cold versus Influenza (Flu): Symptoms, Differences, and Care",
    "content_hash": "f2c2c4dd1bac12f7"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza_2",
    "document_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza",
    "passage_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza:p0002",
    "source_title": "Common Cold versus Influenza (Flu): Symptoms, Differences, and Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/commoncold.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Respiratory Health",
    "section": "Overview",
    "text": "## Overview\nBoth the common cold and influenza (the flu) are contagious viral respiratory infections, but they are caused by different viruses. The common cold is most frequently caused by rhinoviruses, whereas the flu is caused specifically by influenza viruses (primarily types A and B). Because they share overlapping symptoms, distinguishing between them is critical for proper clinical management.",
    "content_hash": "2a85bb7a4da812e3"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza_3",
    "document_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza",
    "passage_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza:p0003",
    "source_title": "Common Cold versus Influenza (Flu): Symptoms, Differences, and Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/commoncold.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Respiratory Health",
    "section": "Comparing Symptoms: Cold vs. Flu",
    "text": "## Comparing Symptoms: Cold vs. Flu\n- **Onset of Symptoms:**\n- *Cold:* Gradual and insidious onset over several days. - *Flu:* Sudden, abrupt onset; patients often recall the exact hour they fell ill. - **Fever:**\n- *Cold:* Rare or low-grade in adults, though mild fevers can occur in young children. - *Flu:* Common and characteristic (often 100°F to 104°F / 37.8°C to 40°C), typically lasting 3 to 4 days. - **Body Aches and Pains:**\n- *Cold:* Slight or absent. - *Flu:* Moderate to severe generalized muscle and joint aches (myalgia), often debilitating. - **Chills and Fatigue:**\n- *Cold:* Mild fatigue and uncommon chills.",
    "content_hash": "cb4cc030b949ac0f"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza_4",
    "document_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza",
    "passage_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza:p0004",
    "source_title": "Common Cold versus Influenza (Flu): Symptoms, Differences, and Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/commoncold.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Respiratory Health",
    "section": "Comparing Symptoms: Cold vs. Flu",
    "text": "- *Flu:* Pronounced shivering chills and profound fatigue/exhaustion that can persist for 2 to 3 weeks. - **Nasal Congestion & Sneezing:**\n- *Cold:* Very common early symptom; prominent runny nose, sneezing, and sore throat. - *Flu:* Sometimes present, but overshadowed by systemic symptoms (fever, aches, cough). - **Cough:**\n- *Cold:* Mild to moderate hacking cough. - *Flu:* Common, dry, deep, and often severe cough that can progress to bronchitis or pneumonia.",
    "content_hash": "ff8c8c70e17d3fe5"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza_5",
    "document_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza",
    "passage_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza:p0005",
    "source_title": "Common Cold versus Influenza (Flu): Symptoms, Differences, and Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/commoncold.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Respiratory Health",
    "section": "Supportive Home Care",
    "text": "## Supportive Home Care\nAntibiotics are completely ineffective against both cold and flu viruses because antibiotics target bacteria only. Recommended supportive management includes:\n- **Rest and Recovery:** Ample sleep to conserve immune energy.\n- **Hydration:** Generous fluid intake (water, clear broths, herbal teas) to thin mucous secretions and prevent fever-induced dehydration.\n- **Symptom Relief:** Warm saline gargles for sore throat, saline nasal sprays or rinses for nasal congestion, and cool-mist room humidifiers.",
    "content_hash": "1740b2fbf686c967"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza_6",
    "document_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza",
    "passage_id": "common_cold_vs_influenza:p0006",
    "source_title": "Common Cold versus Influenza (Flu): Symptoms, Differences, and Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/commoncold.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Respiratory Health",
    "section": "Red Flags & Emergency Medical Signs",
    "text": "## Red Flags & Emergency Medical Signs\nSeek urgent emergency medical evaluation if you or someone under your care experiences:\n- Difficulty breathing, shortness of breath, or persistent chest or abdominal pain.\n- Persistent dizziness, confusion, or inability to be awakened.\n- Inability to keep fluids down, leading to signs of dehydration.\n- Symptoms that initially improved but returned with a worse fever and intensified cough.\n- High risk individuals (infants, adults 65+, pregnant individuals, and those with asthma, COPD, or diabetes) should consult a doctor promptly upon flu symptom onset.",
    "content_hash": "018726c0f45d712e"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage_1",
    "document_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage",
    "passage_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage:p0001",
    "source_title": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
