    "category": "Emergency Medicine & Triage",
    "section": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "text": "# Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "content_hash": "b7ddaa3b07b851da"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage_2",
    "document_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage",
    "passage_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage:p0002",
    "source_title": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001927.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Emergency Medicine & Triage",
    "section": "When to Call Emergency Medical Services Immediately",
    "text": "## When to Call Emergency Medical Services Immediately\nA medical emergency is an acute injury or illness that poses an immediate risk to a person's life or long-term health. When red flag warning signs appear, immediate activation of emergency medical dispatch (such as **112** or **108** in India, **911** in the US, or **999** in the UK) is imperative.",
    "content_hash": "0818bda452d9eb9e"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage_3",
    "document_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage",
    "passage_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage:p0003",
    "source_title": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001927.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Emergency Medicine & Triage",
    "section": "Critical Red Flag Warning Signs",
    "text": "## Critical Red Flag Warning Signs\nMedical professionals recognize several key symptom constellations that necessitate immediate hospital emergency department evaluation:",
    "content_hash": "daf31eb10a0ddd25"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage_4",
    "document_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage",
    "passage_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage:p0004",
    "source_title": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001927.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Emergency Medicine & Triage",
    "section": "1. Chest Pain and Cardiac Warning Signs",
    "text": "### 1. Chest Pain and Cardiac Warning Signs\n- Crushing, heavy pressure, squeezing, or fullness in the center of the chest.\n- Pain radiating to the jaw, neck, back, epigastrium, or down the left or both arms.\n- Chest discomfort accompanied by unexplained shortness of breath, profuse cold sweating (diaphoresis), dizziness, lightheadedness, or sudden nausea.\n- *Clinical rule:* Never drive yourself or wait to see if chest pain dissipates.",
    "content_hash": "ba5acea6fc06584b"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage_5",
    "document_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage",
    "passage_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage:p0005",
    "source_title": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001927.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Emergency Medicine & Triage",
    "section": "2. Acute Stroke Warning Signs (The FAST Protocol)",
    "text": "### 2. Acute Stroke Warning Signs (The FAST Protocol)\n- **F (Face Drooping):** One side of the face droops or is numb. An uneven smile indicates cranial nerve involvement.\n- **A (Arm Weakness):** Sudden weakness or numbness in one arm or leg, or inability to raise both arms evenly.\n- **S (Speech Difficulty):** Slurred speech, inability to repeat simple phrases, or confusion in comprehending spoken language.\n- **T (Time to Call Emergency Services):** Immediate medical intervention within the first 3 to 4.5 hours is critical for thrombolytic or endovascular therapy.",
    "content_hash": "1df851332ae69fb3"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage_6",
    "document_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage",
    "passage_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage:p0006",
    "source_title": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001927.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Emergency Medicine & Triage",
    "section": "3. Severe Respiratory Distress",
    "text": "### 3. Severe Respiratory Distress\n- Severe shortness of breath or sensation of suffocation.\n- Inability to speak in full sentences without gasping for breath.\n- Blue, gray, or pale discoloration of lips, tongue, or nail beds (cyanosis indicating systemic hypoxia).\n- Stridor (high-pitched whistling sound during inhalation indicating upper airway obstruction).",
    "content_hash": "24f9ff670c661168"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage_7",
    "document_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage",
    "passage_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage:p0007",
    "source_title": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001927.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Emergency Medicine & Triage",
    "section": "4. Sudden Severe Neurological Symptoms",
    "text": "### 4. Sudden Severe Neurological Symptoms\n- A sudden, blinding, explosive headache reaching maximum intensity within seconds (termed a \"thunderclap headache\"), which may signal subarachnoid hemorrhage.\n- Severe headache accompanied by a high fever, stiff neck, photophobia, confusion, or a purpuric rash (signs of acute bacterial meningitis).\n- Sudden onset of seizures in an individual without prior seizure disorder.",
    "content_hash": "d7b749113162f2fe"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage_8",
    "document_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage",
    "passage_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage:p0008",
    "source_title": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001927.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Emergency Medicine & Triage",
    "section": "5. Anaphylaxis (Severe Systemic Allergic Reaction)",
    "text": "### 5. Anaphylaxis (Severe Systemic Allergic Reaction)\n- Sudden swelling of the tongue, lips, throat, or uvula.\n- Feeling of the throat constricting or severe difficulty swallowing and breathing.\n- Generalized hives, rapid pulse, dizziness, or collapse following an insect sting, food intake, or medication.",
    "content_hash": "4207eb88df278e7e"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage_9",
    "document_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage",
    "passage_id": "emergency_red_flags_and_triage:p0009",
    "source_title": "Medical Emergencies and Warning Signs Guide",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001927.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Emergency Medicine & Triage",
    "section": "Routine vs. Urgent Medical Consultation",
    "text": "## Routine vs. Urgent Medical Consultation\n- **Routine Consultation:** Mild symptoms of gradual onset (e.g., mild nasal congestion, occasional joint stiffness, minor fatigue) can be addressed via an outpatient scheduled appointment.\n- **Urgent Care / Emergency Department:** Sudden onset, severe pain, progressive worsening, bleeding, high fever unresponsive to antipyretics, or signs of organ compromise warrant immediate clinical evaluation.",
    "content_hash": "4b89859d360060fc"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_1",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0001",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "text": "# Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "content_hash": "7cdfc16244e706ea"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_2",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0002",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "What Causes a Fever",
    "text": "## What Causes a Fever\nA fever is defined as a temporary elevation in normal core body temperature, typically above 100.4°F (38.0°C). It is not an illness itself, but an adaptive physiological response by the immune system. The hypothalamus in the brain acts as the body's thermostat; in response to biochemical substances called pyrogens (released by immune cells or infectious microorganisms), the hypothalamus resets the body's set point to a higher temperature to help fight off infection.",
    "content_hash": "b7e5ea4f85121045"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_3",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0003",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "What Causes a Fever",
    "text": "), the hypothalamus resets the body's set point to a higher temperature to help fight off infection. Common causes of fever include:\n- **Viral Infections:** The most frequent cause, including the common cold, influenza (flu), COVID-19, gastrointestinal viruses (stomach flu), and childhood viral exanthems.\n- **Bacterial Infections:** Conditions such as streptococcal pharyngitis (strep throat), urinary tract infections (UTIs), bacterial pneumonia, ear infections, and skin infections (cellulitis).\n- **Inflammatory & Autoimmune Conditions:** Diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis, lupus, or inflammatory bowel disease.\n- **Immunizations:** Mild, short-lived low-grade fevers frequently occur after childhood or adult vaccinations as the immune system builds protective antibodies.\n- **Environmental & Heat Illness:** Heat exhaustion or heat stroke from prolonged exposure to high ambient temperatures or intense exertion.\n- **Medications:** Drug-induced fever can occur as an adverse reaction to certain antibiotics, anticonvulsants, or antihypertensive drugs.",
    "content_hash": "458388545cca26dd"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_4",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0004",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "Viral versus Bacterial Infections: Key Differences",
    "text": "## Viral versus Bacterial Infections: Key Differences\nDifferentiating viral from bacterial causes is critical for proper medical care:\n- **Causative Agents:** Viruses are microscopic packets of genetic material requiring host cells to replicate, whereas bacteria are independent single-celled organisms.",
    "content_hash": "5d609bc9363b41ba"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_5",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0005",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "Viral versus Bacterial Infections: Key Differences",
    "text": "- **Symptom Patterns:** Viral infections frequently cause widespread systemic symptoms (generalized muscle aches, runny nose, cough, mild sore throat), whereas bacterial infections often present with localized, intense symptoms (e.g., severe unilateral throat pain with exudate in strep throat, localized flank pain with burning urination in kidney infections). - **Treatment Approach:** Antibiotics kill bacteria or stop their growth, but have zero effect against viruses. Using antibiotics for viral fevers contributes to antimicrobial resistance and causes unnecessary drug side effects.",
    "content_hash": "a4230f2a255d5cb2"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_6",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0006",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "Viral versus Bacterial Infections: Key Differences",
    "text": "Antiviral medications exist for select viral infections (such as influenza or COVID-19) when administered early.",
    "content_hash": "2d509edfd19ff523"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_7",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0007",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "Supportive Home Care and Hydration",
    "text": "## Supportive Home Care and Hydration\nMost mild fevers caused by common infections run their course in 2 to 3 days and can be managed with supportive care:\n- **Hydration is Crucial:** Increased body temperature accelerates fluid loss through sweating and rapid respiration. Drink plenty of water, clear broths, oral rehydration solutions (ORS), or electrolyte-rich fluids to prevent dehydration. - **Rest:** Ample sleep and physical rest allow the immune system to marshal metabolic resources toward fighting the pathogen.",
    "content_hash": "deefd6f59422937b"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_8",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0008",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "Supportive Home Care and Hydration",
    "text": "- **Comfort Measures:** Wear lightweight, breathable clothing and maintain room temperature at a comfortable level (around 68°F to 72°F / 20°C to 22°C). Do not bundle up in heavy blankets, as this can trap heat and drive core temperature higher. Use lukewarm sponge baths if uncomfortable; never use cold water, ice baths, or alcohol rubs. - **Over-the-Counter Antipyretics:** Medications such as acetaminophen or ibuprofen may reduce fever and ease body aches when used strictly according to package instructions or clinical guidance.",
    "content_hash": "2339a8e3fa1095b1"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_9",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0009",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "Supportive Home Care and Hydration",
    "text": "- **Critical Pediatric Warning:** Never give aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) to children, teenagers, or young adults recovering from fever or viral illnesses due to the risk of Reye's syndrome, a rare but life-threatening condition causing acute liver damage and cerebral edema.",
    "content_hash": "105f5d4c76d78755"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_10",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0010",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "When to Seek Medical Evaluation (Age-Sensitive Guidelines)",
    "text": "## When to Seek Medical Evaluation (Age-Sensitive Guidelines)\nMedical evaluation is warranted depending on age, temperature, and duration:\n- **Infants under 3 months old:** Any rectal temperature of 100.4°F (38.0°C) or higher requires immediate emergency medical evaluation, even if the infant shows no other symptoms. Young infants have immature immune systems and can rapidly deteriorate from serious bacterial infections.",
    "content_hash": "aa9c320d450ee3a5"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_11",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0011",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "When to Seek Medical Evaluation (Age-Sensitive Guidelines)",
    "text": "- **Children 3 to 24 months old:** Seek prompt pediatric evaluation if fever rises above 102.2°F (39.0°C), lasts longer than 24 to 48 hours, or is accompanied by persistent vomiting, poor oral intake, refusal to drink fluids, or unusual irritability. - **Children over 2 years and Adolescents:** Consult a clinician if the fever repeatedly spikes over 103°F (39.4°C), lasts longer than 3 consecutive days, or fails to respond to antipyretic medication. - **Adults:** Consult a doctor if body temperature exceeds 103°F (39.4°C), if fever persists for more than 3 consecutive days, or if fever repeatedly goes away and returns.",
    "content_hash": "a8d7edcf13f932fb"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_12",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0012",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "Emergency Warning Signs (Red Flags)",
    "text": "## Emergency Warning Signs (Red Flags)\nImmediately seek emergency medical care (call 112 or 108 in India, 911 in the US, or proceed to the nearest emergency department) if fever is accompanied by any of the following critical red flags:\n- **Stiff Neck & Confusion:** Inability to bend the neck forward (nuchal rigidity), severe explosive headache, photophobia (sensitivity to light), confusion, or altered mental status—these are hallmarks of acute bacterial meningitis. - **Respiratory Distress:** Extreme shortness of breath, rapid labored breathing, wheezing, or bluish discoloration of the lips or face (cyanosis).",
    "content_hash": "c38b230512f8de0f"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fever_causes_and_management_13",
    "document_id": "fever_causes_and_management",
    "passage_id": "fever_causes_and_management:p0013",
    "source_title": "Fever Causes, Symptoms, and When to Seek Medical Care",
    "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) & MedlinePlus (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/fever.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Infectious Diseases & Clinical Triage",
    "section": "Emergency Warning Signs (Red Flags)",
    "text": "- **Severe Dehydration:** Inability to retain fluids, complete absence of urination for 8+ hours, severe dizziness when standing, dry mucous membranes, or crying without tears in young children. - **New Unexplained Rash:** A sudden purple, red, or petechial rash (pinpoint spots that do not fade when pressed under a clear glass). - **Neurological Symptoms:** Seizures (febrile convulsions or status epilepticus), extreme lethargy, or difficulty waking the person.",
    "content_hash": "0dece7f9d6e25a53"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition_1",
    "document_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition",
    "passage_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition:p0001",
    "source_title": "Healthy Diet and Nutrition Facts",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Nutritional Science & Preventive Health",
    "section": "Healthy Diet and Nutrition Facts",
    "text": "# Healthy Diet and Nutrition Facts",
    "content_hash": "7efdedccf2a9c30f"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition_2",
    "document_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition",
    "passage_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition:p0002",
    "source_title": "Healthy Diet and Nutrition Facts",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Nutritional Science & Preventive Health",
    "section": "WHO Dietary Guidelines for Adults",
    "text": "## WHO Dietary Guidelines for Adults\nA healthy diet protects against malnutrition in all its forms, as well as noncommunicable diseases (NCDs) such as diabetes, heart disease, stroke, and cancer. The World Health Organization establishes the following evidence-based nutritional principles:",
    "content_hash": "ebca211c1ff6d08c"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition_3",
    "document_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition",
    "passage_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition:p0003",
    "source_title": "Healthy Diet and Nutrition Facts",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Nutritional Science & Preventive Health",
    "section": "Core Food Groups",
    "text": "### Core Food Groups\n- **Fruit, vegetables, legumes, nuts, and whole grains:**\n- Consume at least 400 grams (approximately 5 portions) of fruits and vegetables daily, excluding starchy roots such as potatoes or cassava.\n- Choose unprocessed whole grains (e.g., oats, whole wheat, brown rice, quinoa, millet) rather than refined grains.",
    "content_hash": "048706442d847806"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition_4",
    "document_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition",
    "passage_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition:p0004",
    "source_title": "Healthy Diet and Nutrition Facts",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Nutritional Science & Preventive Health",
    "section": "Fats and Lipids",
    "text": "### Fats and Lipids\n- Total fat intake should not exceed 30% of total energy intake to prevent unhealthy weight gain.\n- Prefer unsaturated fats (found in fish, avocados, nuts, and sunflower, soybean, canola, and olive oils) over saturated fats (found in fatty meat, butter, palm and coconut oil, cream, cheese, and lard).\n- Saturated fats should represent less than 10% of total caloric intake.\n- Industrial trans-fats (found in fried foods, commercial baked goods, and pre-packaged frozen snacks) should be eliminated completely (less than 1% of total energy).",
    "content_hash": "adfeea884917edbf"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition_5",
    "document_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition",
    "passage_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition:p0005",
    "source_title": "Healthy Diet and Nutrition Facts",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Nutritional Science & Preventive Health",
    "section": "Free Sugars",
    "text": "### Free Sugars\n- Free sugar intake should be reduced to less than 10% of total daily energy intake (equivalent to roughly 50 grams or about 12 level teaspoons for a 2,000-calorie diet).\n- Further reduction to below 5% of total energy provides additional proven health benefits, specifically preventing dental caries and metabolic disorders.\n- Free sugars include all monosaccharides and disaccharides added to foods and drinks by manufacturers or cooks, plus sugars naturally present in honey, syrups, fruit juices, and fruit juice concentrates.",
    "content_hash": "631b26c1b26ab38c"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition_6",
    "document_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition",
    "passage_id": "healthy_diet_and_nutrition:p0006",
    "source_title": "Healthy Diet and Nutrition Facts",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Nutritional Science & Preventive Health",
    "section": "Salt, Sodium, and Potassium",
    "text": "### Salt, Sodium, and Potassium\n- Limit salt consumption to less than 5 grams per day (approximately 1 level teaspoon), which corresponds to under 2,000 mg of elemental sodium.\n- Ensure all dietary salt is iodized to support normal thyroid hormone synthesis.\n- Increase dietary potassium intake (at least 3,510 mg per day) by eating dark leafy greens, pulses, nuts, and fresh fruits to help mitigate blood pressure elevations.",
    "content_hash": "838030c5a4475b10"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hydration_and_water_balance_1",
    "document_id": "hydration_and_water_balance",
    "passage_id": "hydration_and_water_balance:p0001",
    "source_title": "Water and Daily Hydration Requirements",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002471.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Physiology & Preventive Wellness",
    "section": "Water and Daily Hydration Requirements",
    "text": "# Water and Daily Hydration Requirements",
    "content_hash": "468b30d8e001882d"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hydration_and_water_balance_2",
    "document_id": "hydration_and_water_balance",
    "passage_id": "hydration_and_water_balance:p0002",
    "source_title": "Water and Daily Hydration Requirements",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002471.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Physiology & Preventive Wellness",
    "section": "Why Water is Essential",
    "text": "## Why Water is Essential\nWater accounts for approximately 55% to 60% of an adult human body weight. It plays essential roles in every organ system:\n- Regulating core body temperature through sweating and respiration.\n- Lubricating and cushioning joints and spinal discs.\n- Transporting dissolved nutrients, oxygen, and hormones to cellular tissues.\n- Assisting renal clearance of waste metabolites and nitrogenous compounds.\n- Preventing constipation and facilitating gastrointestinal motility.",
    "content_hash": "1eae9a6ce40c3b09"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hydration_and_water_balance_3",
    "document_id": "hydration_and_water_balance",
    "passage_id": "hydration_and_water_balance:p0003",
    "source_title": "Water and Daily Hydration Requirements",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002471.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Physiology & Preventive Wellness",
    "section": "Recommended Fluid Intake",
    "text": "## Recommended Fluid Intake\nWhile individual water needs fluctuate depending on climate, temperature, physical exertion, and overall health status, general health guidelines suggest:\n- **Women:** Approximately 2.7 liters (about 11 cups) of total fluid per day from all beverages and moisture-rich foods. - **Men:** Approximately 3.7 liters (about 15.5 cups) of total fluid per day from all beverages and moisture-rich foods.",
    "content_hash": "e837788004268a6e"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hydration_and_water_balance_4",
    "document_id": "hydration_and_water_balance",
    "passage_id": "hydration_and_water_balance:p0004",
    "source_title": "Water and Daily Hydration Requirements",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002471.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Physiology & Preventive Wellness",
    "section": "Recommended Fluid Intake",
    "text": "- Roughly 20% of daily water intake typically comes from solid foods (especially fruits and vegetables such as cucumbers, watermelons, tomatoes, and oranges), with the remaining 80% provided by drinking water and non-sugary beverages.",
    "content_hash": "8f0bd568b9e3d38b"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hydration_and_water_balance_5",
    "document_id": "hydration_and_water_balance",
    "passage_id": "hydration_and_water_balance:p0005",
    "source_title": "Water and Daily Hydration Requirements",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002471.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Physiology & Preventive Wellness",
    "section": "Recognizing Signs of Dehydration",
    "text": "## Recognizing Signs of Dehydration\n- **Early / Mild Dehydration:** Thirst, dark amber or cloudy urine, reduced urination frequency, dry mouth, mild lightheadedness, fatigue, and tension headaches.\n- **Severe Dehydration (Medical Emergency):** Extreme thirst, sunken eyes, rapid heart rate (tachycardia), low blood pressure (hypotension), confusion or lethargy, lack of sweating despite heat, and minimal or no urine output for over 8 hours.",
    "content_hash": "beccdfb9dad63790"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hydration_and_water_balance_6",
    "document_id": "hydration_and_water_balance",
    "passage_id": "hydration_and_water_balance:p0006",
    "source_title": "Water and Daily Hydration Requirements",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002471.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Physiology & Preventive Wellness",
    "section": "Practical Strategies for Consistent Hydration",
    "text": "## Practical Strategies for Consistent Hydration\n- Keep a reusable water bottle visible on your desk or when traveling. - Drink a glass of water upon waking to replenish fluids lost through nighttime respiration. - Infuse water with natural flavorings such as lemon slices, cucumber, mint leaves, or berries if plain water tastes bland. - Drink water before, during, and after strenuous physical exertion or time spent in hot environments. - Monitor your urine color: pale straw or light yellow signifies optimal hydration, while dark yellow indicates a need for fluids.",
    "content_hash": "1e22c34150114152"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hydration_and_water_balance_7",
    "document_id": "hydration_and_water_balance",
    "passage_id": "hydration_and_water_balance:p0007",
    "source_title": "Water and Daily Hydration Requirements",
    "source": "MedlinePlus & World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002471.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Physiology & Preventive Wellness",
    "section": "Practical Strategies for Consistent Hydration",
    "text": "- Caution for special conditions: Individuals with congestive heart failure, chronic kidney disease (CKD), or severe liver cirrhosis should adhere strictly to their physician's individualized fluid limits.",
    "content_hash": "552316986ae1c754"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health_1",
    "document_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health",
    "passage_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health:p0001",
    "source_title": "Hypertension and Cardiovascular Health Guidelines",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Cardiology & Vascular Health",
    "section": "Hypertension and Cardiovascular Health Guidelines",
    "text": "# Hypertension and Cardiovascular Health Guidelines",
    "content_hash": "8649cce5790da248"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health_2",
    "document_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health",
    "passage_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health:p0002",
    "source_title": "Hypertension and Cardiovascular Health Guidelines",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Cardiology & Vascular Health",
    "section": "What is Hypertension?",
    "text": "## What is Hypertension?\nHypertension (high blood pressure) is a chronic medical condition in which the systemic arterial blood pressure is persistently elevated. Blood pressure is recorded as two numbers:\n- **Systolic pressure (top number):** The pressure in blood vessels when the heart contracts or beats.\n- **Diastolic pressure (bottom number):** The pressure in the vessels when the heart rests between beats.",
    "content_hash": "fc5c580b802bbfe1"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health_3",
    "document_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health",
    "passage_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health:p0003",
    "source_title": "Hypertension and Cardiovascular Health Guidelines",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Cardiology & Vascular Health",
    "section": "What is Hypertension?",
    "text": "iastolic pressure (bottom number):** The pressure in the vessels when the heart rests between beats. Hypertension is diagnosed when measured on two different days is systolic blood pressure on both days is ≥140 mmHg and/or the diastolic blood pressure on both days is ≥90 mmHg according to WHO guidelines (or ≥130/80 mmHg under AHA/ACC guidelines).",
    "content_hash": "ac8c8554dd5cdffd"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health_4",
    "document_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health",
    "passage_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health:p0004",
    "source_title": "Hypertension and Cardiovascular Health Guidelines",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Cardiology & Vascular Health",
    "section": "The \"Silent Killer\"",
    "text": "## The \"Silent Killer\"\nHypertension is often referred to as a \"silent killer\" because the vast majority of people with high blood pressure experience no noticeable warning signs or symptoms. Over time, untreated high blood pressure damages arterial walls, significantly escalating the risk of stroke, myocardial infarction (heart attack), heart failure, kidney disease, and vascular dementia.",
    "content_hash": "3bd84588a983a0ef"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health_5",
    "document_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health",
    "passage_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health:p0005",
    "source_title": "Hypertension and Cardiovascular Health Guidelines",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Cardiology & Vascular Health",
    "section": "Modifiable Risk Factors and Lifestyle Modifications",
    "text": "## Modifiable Risk Factors and Lifestyle Modifications\nThe World Health Organization highlights key evidence-based interventions to prevent and control hypertension:\n- **Sodium Restriction:** Reduce daily dietary sodium intake to less than 2,000 mg per day (equivalent to about 5 grams or 1 teaspoon of table salt). Avoid heavily processed meats, canned soups, instant noodles, and packaged snack foods. - **Heart-Healthy Nutrition (DASH Diet):** Emphasize vegetables, fresh fruits, whole grains, legumes, lean poultry, and nuts. Increase dietary potassium (bananas, spinach, sweet potatoes, avocados), which counters sodium's hypertensive effects.",
    "content_hash": "2aafe5a23fe0dfcc"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health_6",
    "document_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health",
    "passage_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health:p0006",
    "source_title": "Hypertension and Cardiovascular Health Guidelines",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Cardiology & Vascular Health",
    "section": "Modifiable Risk Factors and Lifestyle Modifications",
    "text": "- **Healthy Weight Maintenance:** Losing even 5% to 10% of excess body weight can produce substantial reductions in both systolic and diastolic blood pressure. - **Regular Aerobic Physical Exercise:** Engage in at least 150 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic exercise (e.g., brisk walking, swimming, cycling) per week. - **Limiting Alcohol and Quitting Tobacco:** Tobacco use causes immediate acute vasoconstriction and chronic atherosclerosis. Alcohol consumption should be moderated or eliminated. - **Stress Management:** Incorporate relaxation routines and adequate sleep (7–9 hours nightly).",
    "content_hash": "87dc952d0ff4ac15"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health_7",
    "document_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health",
    "passage_id": "hypertension_and_heart_health:p0007",
    "source_title": "Hypertension and Cardiovascular Health Guidelines",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO) & MedlinePlus",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Cardiology & Vascular Health",
    "section": "When to Seek Urgent Clinical Attention",
    "text": "## When to Seek Urgent Clinical Attention\nA blood pressure reading exceeding 180/120 mmHg is classified as a **Hypertensive Crisis**. If accompanied by chest pressure, shortness of breath, back pain, numbness, vision changes, or difficulty speaking, emergency medical services must be contacted immediately.",
    "content_hash": "8fd979e3b01c1f12"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "physical_activity_guidelines_1",
    "document_id": "physical_activity_guidelines",
    "passage_id": "physical_activity_guidelines:p0001",
    "source_title": "Global Recommendations on Physical Activity for Health",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Exercise Physiology & Public Health",
    "section": "Global Recommendations on Physical Activity for Health",
    "text": "# Global Recommendations on Physical Activity for Health",
    "content_hash": "c8827ed4f4e84d7d"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "physical_activity_guidelines_2",
    "document_id": "physical_activity_guidelines",
    "passage_id": "physical_activity_guidelines:p0002",
    "source_title": "Global Recommendations on Physical Activity for Health",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Exercise Physiology & Public Health",
    "section": "Definition and Scope",
    "text": "## Definition and Scope\nPhysical activity refers to all bodily movement produced by skeletal muscles that requires energy expenditure. This includes exercise as well as physical activities undertaken while working, active transportation (walking, cycling), doing household chores, and participating in recreational sports.",
    "content_hash": "15aed9b1f0c07336"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "physical_activity_guidelines_3",
    "document_id": "physical_activity_guidelines",
    "passage_id": "physical_activity_guidelines:p0003",
    "source_title": "Global Recommendations on Physical Activity for Health",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Exercise Physiology & Public Health",
    "section": "Recommended Levels for Adults (Aged 18–64 Years)",
    "text": "## Recommended Levels for Adults (Aged 18–64 Years)\nTo achieve substantial health benefits and reduce the risks of all-cause mortality, cardiovascular diseases, hypertension, type 2 diabetes, depression, anxiety, and cognitive decline, the WHO recommends:\n- **Aerobic Activity:**\n- At least 150 to 300 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic physical activity per week (e.g., brisk walking, easy cycling, dancing, active gardening); OR\n- At least 75 to 150 minutes of vigorous-intensity aerobic physical activity per week (e.g., running, fast cycling, lap swimming, high-intensity interval training); OR\n- An equivalent combination of moderate- and vigorous-intensity activity throughout the week.",
    "content_hash": "8c06d1cbc680821c"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "physical_activity_guidelines_4",
    "document_id": "physical_activity_guidelines",
    "passage_id": "physical_activity_guidelines:p0004",
    "source_title": "Global Recommendations on Physical Activity for Health",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Exercise Physiology & Public Health",
    "section": "Recommended Levels for Adults (Aged 18–64 Years)",
    "text": "- **Muscle-Strengthening Activities:**\n- Muscle-strengthening activities involving all major muscle groups (legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders, and arms) on 2 or more days per week. - **Limiting Sedentary Behavior:**\n- Replace sedentary time (prolonged sitting, screen time) with physical activity of any intensity, including light intensity. Any physical activity is significantly better than none.",
    "content_hash": "a26cdbb944dae83c"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "physical_activity_guidelines_5",
    "document_id": "physical_activity_guidelines",
    "passage_id": "physical_activity_guidelines:p0005",
    "source_title": "Global Recommendations on Physical Activity for Health",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Exercise Physiology & Public Health",
    "section": "Older Adults (Aged 65 Years and Above)",
    "text": "## Older Adults (Aged 65 Years and Above)\n- Older adults should follow the same adult aerobic and muscle-strengthening guidelines, with an added emphasis on **multicomponent functional balance and strength training** at moderate or greater intensity on 3 or more days per week.\n- Balance and coordination exercises help prevent falls and reduce fall-related fractures and injuries.",
    "content_hash": "892d14c76b78c4c0"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "physical_activity_guidelines_6",
    "document_id": "physical_activity_guidelines",
    "passage_id": "physical_activity_guidelines:p0006",
    "source_title": "Global Recommendations on Physical Activity for Health",
    "source": "World Health Organization (WHO)",
    "source_url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Exercise Physiology & Public Health",
    "section": "Health Benefits of Regular Movement",
    "text": "## Health Benefits of Regular Movement\n- Enhances muscular fitness, joint mobility, and bone density.\n- Lowers risk of stroke, coronary heart disease, and hypertension.\n- Improves insulin sensitivity and glycemic control.\n- Enhances mental wellbeing, alleviates stress, and promotes deeper nocturnal sleep quality.",
    "content_hash": "d18b59410730af3a"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders_1",
    "document_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders",
    "passage_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders:p0001",
    "source_title": "Healthy Sleep Habits and Insomnia Overview",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthysleep.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Sleep Medicine & Lifestyle",
    "section": "Healthy Sleep Habits and Insomnia Overview",
    "text": "# Healthy Sleep Habits and Insomnia Overview",
    "content_hash": "d1099d9ca31be99f"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders_2",
    "document_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders",
    "passage_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders:p0002",
    "source_title": "Healthy Sleep Habits and Insomnia Overview",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthysleep.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Sleep Medicine & Lifestyle",
    "section": "Introduction to Healthy Sleep",
    "text": "## Introduction to Healthy Sleep\nSleep is a vital biological function that allows the body and brain to recover, consolidate memory, and maintain hormonal and cardiovascular health. Most healthy adults require between 7 to 9 hours of quality, restful sleep per night. Children and adolescents require more sleep, ranging from 8 to 14 hours depending on age.",
    "content_hash": "b68d8c0a44d06c84"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders_3",
    "document_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders",
    "passage_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders:p0003",
    "source_title": "Healthy Sleep Habits and Insomnia Overview",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthysleep.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Sleep Medicine & Lifestyle",
    "section": "Principles of Sleep Hygiene",
    "text": "## Principles of Sleep Hygiene\nGood sleep hygiene consists of behavioral and environmental practices that promote consistent, uninterrupted sleep:\n- **Consistent Schedule:** Go to bed and wake up at the exact same time every day, including on weekends and holidays. This synchronizes your circadian biological clock. - **Sleep Environment:** Keep the bedroom quiet, dark, and cool (typically around 65°F to 68°F or 18°C to 20°C). Use blackout curtains or an eye mask if ambient light is unavoidable. - **Screen and Blue Light Restrictions:** Turn off smartphones, computers, tablets, and televisions at least 60 minutes before bedtime.",
    "content_hash": "02462da563862159"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders_4",
    "document_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders",
    "passage_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders:p0004",
    "source_title": "Healthy Sleep Habits and Insomnia Overview",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthysleep.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Sleep Medicine & Lifestyle",
    "section": "Principles of Sleep Hygiene",
    "text": "Electronic screens emit short-wavelength blue light that suppresses pineal melatonin production, delaying sleep onset. - **Caffeine and Stimulant Management:** Avoid consuming caffeinated beverages (coffee, energy drinks, black or green tea, colas) at least 6 hours before bedtime. - **Alcohol and Meals:** Avoid heavy, spicy, or high-sugar meals within 3 hours of sleep, which can cause indigestion and sleep disruption. While alcohol is a sedative that might accelerate falling asleep, it severely impairs REM sleep architecture and causes frequent nighttime awakenings.",
    "content_hash": "0067518fdffac25d"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders_5",
    "document_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders",
    "passage_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders:p0005",
    "source_title": "Healthy Sleep Habits and Insomnia Overview",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthysleep.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Sleep Medicine & Lifestyle",
    "section": "Principles of Sleep Hygiene",
    "text": "- **Regular Daytime Physical Activity:** Engaging in moderate physical exercise earlier in the day improves sleep depth, but vigorous cardiovascular workouts should be completed at least 2 to 3 hours before retiring.",
    "content_hash": "1877991b36605f3a"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders_6",
    "document_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders",
    "passage_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders:p0006",
    "source_title": "Healthy Sleep Habits and Insomnia Overview",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthysleep.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Sleep Medicine & Lifestyle",
    "section": "Common Sleep Disorders",
    "text": "## Common Sleep Disorders\n- **Insomnia:** Difficulty falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking up too early feeling unrefreshed. Acute insomnia is often triggered by transient stress or schedule disruptions, while chronic insomnia persists for 3 months or longer.\n- **Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA):** A condition characterized by repeated partial or complete airway collapse during sleep, resulting in pauses in breathing, loud snoring, gasping, and severe daytime exhaustion.\n- **Restless Legs Syndrome (RLS):** An uncontrollable urge to move the legs, particularly during evening hours or periods of inactivity.",
    "content_hash": "4761081c545d7132"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders_7",
    "document_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders",
    "passage_id": "sleep_hygiene_and_disorders:p0007",
    "source_title": "Healthy Sleep Habits and Insomnia Overview",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthysleep.html",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Sleep Medicine & Lifestyle",
    "section": "When to Seek Medical Evaluation",
    "text": "## When to Seek Medical Evaluation\nConsult a physician or sleep specialist if:\n- Sleep difficulties persist for longer than 4 weeks and interfere with daytime productivity or mood.\n- You or your bed partner observe gasping, choking, or breathing cessation during sleep.\n- You experience severe excessive daytime sleepiness, such as falling asleep while driving or talking.",
    "content_hash": "9a8f8cc6c4408d3e"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines_1",
    "document_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines",
    "passage_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines:p0001",
    "source_title": "Tension Headaches and Migraines: Causes, Symptoms, and Management",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000797.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Neurology & Pain Management",
    "section": "Tension Headaches and Migraines: Causes, Symptoms, and Management",
    "text": "# Tension Headaches and Migraines: Causes, Symptoms, and Management",
    "content_hash": "5567bfebbed9f456"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines_2",
    "document_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines",
    "passage_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines:p0002",
    "source_title": "Tension Headaches and Migraines: Causes, Symptoms, and Management",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000797.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Neurology & Pain Management",
    "section": "Understanding Tension Headaches",
    "text": "## Understanding Tension Headaches\nTension headaches (also termed tension-type headaches or stress headaches) are the most common primary headache disorder. They are characterized by a dull, aching, non-pulsatile pain that affects both sides of the head. Patients frequently describe the sensation as a tight band or vice constricting the forehead, temples, or back of the neck and scalp.",
    "content_hash": "ad7b9ee7add0a3d8"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines_3",
    "document_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines",
    "passage_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines:p0003",
    "source_title": "Tension Headaches and Migraines: Causes, Symptoms, and Management",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000797.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Neurology & Pain Management",
    "section": "Common Triggers and Contributing Factors",
    "text": "## Common Triggers and Contributing Factors\n- **Psychological and Emotional Stress:** Chronic daily stressors, anxiety, emotional strain, and depression frequently provoke muscular contractions in the head and neck muscles. - **Physical Muscle Strain & Posture:** Prolonged poor ergonomics, such as slouching at a desk, looking down at mobile phones for hours (\"tech neck\"), or holding a phone between the shoulder and ear. - **Ocular Strain:** Uncorrected vision impairments or prolonged glare and screen exposure without visual breaks (the 20-20-20 rule helps: look 20 feet away every 20 minutes for 20 seconds).",
    "content_hash": "f0832e33e720045c"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines_4",
    "document_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines",
    "passage_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines:p0004",
    "source_title": "Tension Headaches and Migraines: Causes, Symptoms, and Management",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000797.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Neurology & Pain Management",
    "section": "Common Triggers and Contributing Factors",
    "text": "- **Physical Deprivation:** Dehydration, missed or irregular meals causing hypoglycemia, and sleep deprivation. - **Caffeine Fluctuations:** Sudden cessation of habitual caffeine intake (caffeine withdrawal).",
    "content_hash": "b10995621f4b0fef"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines_5",
    "document_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines",
    "passage_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines:p0005",
    "source_title": "Tension Headaches and Migraines: Causes, Symptoms, and Management",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000797.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Neurology & Pain Management",
    "section": "Difference Between Tension Headaches and Migraines",
    "text": "## Difference Between Tension Headaches and Migraines\n- **Tension Headache:** Typically bilateral, mild-to-moderate dull ache, not aggravated by routine physical activity, and usually absent of severe nausea, vomiting, or visual auras.\n- **Migraine:** Typically unilateral (one-sided), moderate-to-severe throbbing or pulsating pain, worsens with normal physical activity (walking, stairs), and often accompanied by sensitivity to light (photophobia), sound (phonophobia), nausea, and occasionally sensory/visual auras.",
    "content_hash": "c641101fc939dc83"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines_6",
    "document_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines",
    "passage_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines:p0006",
    "source_title": "Tension Headaches and Migraines: Causes, Symptoms, and Management",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000797.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Neurology & Pain Management",
    "section": "Non-Pharmacological Home Remedies & Preventive Measures",
    "text": "## Non-Pharmacological Home Remedies & Preventive Measures\n- **Heat or Cold Therapy:** Applying a warm heating pad or warm towel to the neck and shoulders eases tight muscles; a cool gel pack to the forehead can relieve dull aching.\n- **Ergonomic Adjustments:** Adjusting chair height, monitor distance (eye level), and using an ergonomic keyboard and mouse.\n- **Hydration & Regular Meals:** Consuming water steadily throughout the day and eating balanced meals to stabilize blood glucose.\n- **Stress Reduction Techniques:** Progressive muscle relaxation (PMR), guided diaphragmatic breathing, meditation, and gentle cervical spine stretches.",
    "content_hash": "4821e0eb796bf20a"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines_7",
    "document_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines",
    "passage_id": "tension_headaches_and_migraines:p0007",
    "source_title": "Tension Headaches and Migraines: Causes, Symptoms, and Management",
    "source": "MedlinePlus, National Library of Medicine (NIH)",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/000797.htm",
    "retrieval_date": "2026-10-21",
    "version": "1",
    "category": "Neurology & Pain Management",
    "section": "Red Flag Symptoms (Seek Emergency Care)",
    "text": "## Red Flag Symptoms (Seek Emergency Care)\nSeek immediate emergency medical attention if a headache is:\n- Sudden and explosively severe, reaching peak intensity within seconds (\"thunderclap headache\").\n- Accompanied by high fever, stiff neck, confusion, seizure, double vision, weakness, numbness, or difficulty speaking.\n- New onset following a head injury, fall, or blunt trauma.\n- Progressively worsening over days or weeks in individuals over 50 years old.",
    "content_hash": "faa9f70954740d05"
  }
]